 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
   ABSTRACT
 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

        The cryptography here is correct. The anchor and the clock are not.

        Verification is genuine: the [ed25519_verify] hook fires on every
        startup with arg2=0x40 and the public-key argument equal to the string
        we swapped in, and the verifier's result feeds the authorization state.
        (Honest limit: the "bad signature => code=4" branch was located
        statically in sub_1404A06F8 but never captured dynamically.)
        So this bypass does NOT patch the verifier and does NOT invert a
        comparison. It goes around the signature entirely:

          [1] TRUST ANCHOR
              The baked-in license public key is a 64-byte plaintext hex
              ASCII constant in .rdata (v0.28.1: file offset 0xBF647B, VA 0x140BF7E7B;
               9/30 baseline: 0xB98733/0x140B99733; value unchanged across
               versions). One object,
              four xrefs, exactly the same length as any Ed25519 key we
              generate. Swap it in place:
                 .reloc  -> untouched      section table -> untouched
                 VA map  -> untouched      PE integrity  -> untouched
              60 of the 64 bytes actually differ. That is the whole patch.

          [2] RE-SIGN
              Mint license.sig with our own key, in the target's own format:
              103-byte .rdata header template +
                 schema / host / path / date / digest / signature / body_b64
              signing base (strict "headers" order):
                 "host: H\n" "date: D\n" "digest: sha-256=<b64(sha256(body))>"
              algorithm string is the literal "ed25519".

          [3] TIME BASE
              Offline grace deadline = signed `date` header + 1209600 (14d),
              computed in fill_result (sub_1406F02C0; arithmetic at 0x140740257,
              LE32 at file 0x73F657; v0.28.1; 9/30 baseline: LE64 at 0x1406F030B)
              -- the constant 0x127500 appears EXACTLY ONCE in the entire binary.
              No skew check. The date is covered only by OUR signature.
                 +3650 d -> UI reports ~3663 d of offline grace remaining
                 -30 d   -> code=2 (grace passed)   [negative control]

          [4] REVOCATION
              Online re-validation is fail-open: unreachable server or
              untrusted reply logs a WARN and leaves local state untouched.
              Nothing the vendor can do through normal revocation pulls the
              grant back.

 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -
   THE GRACE-ANCHOR HUNT (three hypotheses, two of them wrong)
 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -

        T1  deadline = ini last_validated + 14d   -> shift it, UI unchanged  ✗
        T2  deadline = ini activated     + 14d    -> shift it, UI unchanged  ✗
        T3  deadline = signed date hdr   + 14d    -> moves 1:1               ✓

        T1/T2 look plausible -- last_validated is literally "the last time we
        checked". It is a DISPLAY field, not a decision input.

        Control that makes T3 airtight: keep the license key, uuid and body
        BYTE-IDENTICAL across runs and vary only one date source, then
        relaunch. ini fields -> nothing moves; the signed header -> everything
        moves. Without that control, "new license => new timer" explains T1/T2
        away and T3 is only correlation.

        Device binding, for completeness:
           fp = lowercase hex of FNV-1a-64(MachineGuid)  -- 16 chars, NO "0x"
           compared byte-exact BEFORE verification (mismatch => code=6)
           stored in the UNSIGNED ini next to the value it protects
        Key-less hash + plaintext twin = binding in name only.

        Bonus branch: expiry is only consulted when updates maps to 3
        (sub_1404A60EB: strict->0 rollback->1 lifetime->2 else->3).
        So updates=lifetime skips the expiry check entirely. The most
        permissive policy value doubles as the "ignore dates" switch.

